ur  environment acts like a dimmer switch to  change the expression of our genes the  amount of enzyme in our brain for  instance in a certain place and  and it goes up and down and that depends  on our early experience and also our  experience as we go through through  adolescence so we can think about these  gene-environment interactions in that  way and we've been studying the  mechanism and the next five years we're  trying to understand how these molecules  are regulated in the developing brain  how the body is talking to the brain and  how we arrived at individual differences  according to the susceptibility genes  and this epigenetic phenomenon and and  then finally the other big area in our  group has to do with critical or  sensitive periods and development and  this is pioneered by takeo hench who is  at our meeting from Harvard University  and you may know that our brains are  like sponges right we always say oh my  two year olds brain is like a sponge and  really our brains have windows of  opportunity that open and close and  during development when you if you're  pregnant and you're let's say six months  pregnant around that time if you play  that baby music my son would say play  the baby jazz some of you might like  Baca and others might like rock and that  once that baby comes out it can  distinguish between the type of music  that it's hurt or if you play from Janet  workers work in British Columbia if you  play different sounds from from your  language like da English or a dog which  I can't say in Hindi but it sounds  different when that baby emerges it will  have been had that exposure about  language and it will know the difference  between those sounds and then that  window of opportunity closes and by the  time you get like me and you try and  learn another language for sure you're  going to have an accent so there's  windows of opportunities for our for  listening for seeing for language and  also for social behavior and so later on  children learn how to regulate their  social behavior how to have social  cognition how to read social situations  and we know that this window that opens  and closes is regulated by molecules  that to cow and others have discovered  and he and others are able to open that  window closed it make it longer and make  it shorter and and he and others think  that this  windows these windows and brain  development and this and information  coming in may have something to do with  how that brain is wired differently or  not and so that's another very  interesting area of relevance so when  one I'm just going to finish up now so  when one thinks about normal or typical  variation in all these different traits  that have to do with autism we if one  thinks about that we all know people who  are more shy socially or check their  door before they leave several times I  mean with me it's always where's my key  and some of us are better communicating  verbally as compared to in writing and I  think if we graph the abilities of the  people in this room looking at many of  these different characteristics that we  are associating with autism at least in  a quantitative way we would see that  there's no one control there's nothing  that is okay we're going to compare it  to that in fact we have this huge  distribution and then the same is true  for the autistic kids right it's the  same kind of huge variation from one end  to the other in the spectrum and then  we're trying to figure out well what is  a control who should we be comparing  with whom in our experiments and so I  think one of the things we've we've  realized in the last few days is we need  to pay particular attention to the way  we phenotype that is how we quantify  these traits not just quality in that  you have it or you don't but what are  the how do we measure those things and I  lost my train of thought and I supposed  to do that when you're talking in public  but anyways so you're right and so so  that would that should be informed and  not only that but we don't only want to  quantify where those individuals sit in  the typical atypical population but we  know things change in development you're  on a developmental trajectory and so  there's a developmental way of thinking  that something happens at this time  that's going to fact what happens later  and that's true with behavior and brain  development and all those time periods  interact so that in the end you have you  have a grown-up human being and so these  this way of thinking which doesn't  particularly focus on autism or  schizophrenia  alzheimers how the the typical brain  develops and the requirement for that  development to optimize health and  promote optimal well-being for every  child which is one of the focuses of our  group those findings and the things that  we're discussing I think have been  really relevant and exciting and it's  been great to interact with the  clinicians and the researchers
 

